          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, is on four heart-failure medicines, and her ankles are swelling again. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about those drug classes?",
          "answer": "**How urgent:** The symptom check rated this as routine. It matched none of its emergency patterns. That is not a clearance, and the tool cannot assess your mother. Swelling that has returned in someone with heart failure is worth a call to her heart team soon, not something to wait out. The diet guidance describes rising weight and swelling as the usual prompt to contact the care team, and says any written action plan from her clinicians takes priority (\"Diet and fluid in heart failure\"). If she has new or worsening breathlessness, chest pain, confusion, fainting, or trouble breathing when lying flat, seek urgent care straight away. The retrieved material did not give a full list of warning signs, so check her action plan or ask her clinician for one.\n\n**What the guidance says about her medicines:** The retrieved passages only partly answer this.\n\n- The heart-failure guidance (\"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes\") says that for reduced ejection fraction (40% or below), current guidance describes four foundational classes, usually started together or in quick succession rather than one at a time over months. The excerpt I received does not name the four classes, so I cannot tell you which ones she takes.\n- The same guidance notes that the SGLT-2 inhibitor class is the newest addition and has shown benefit in people with preserved ejection fraction, which the other classes have not shown to the same degree.\n- The diabetes guidance (\"Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management\") says that where heart failure is present, particularly with reduced ejection fraction, agents with proven organ benefit are preferred, and it names SGLT-2 inhibitors among them.\n- The diet guidance says potassium supplements and salt substitutes containing potassium chloride interact with several heart-failure medicines, so they should be decided with her clinician.\n\nHer medicines and doses are a matter for her own clinicians. Bring the swelling and any weight changes to her next contact with them, or sooner if she is unwell.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
